"health_atlas_capillary_support_surface_companion" = "Model circumference: %1$@ µm";
"health_atlas_capillary_support_surface_formula" = "The side of the cylinder unrolls into a rectangle. Its width is the circumference 2πr and its length is the segment length L. Side area = 2πr × L, excluding both circular ends. Increasing either radius or length while holding the other fixed increases the side area.";
"health_atlas_capillary_support_surface_length" = "Model segment length";
"health_atlas_capillary_support_surface_radius" = "Model radius";
"health_atlas_capillary_support_surface_scope" = "Pericytes lie along the outside of capillary walls, next to the endothelium. In the kidney glomerulus, mesangial cells and their surrounding matrix provide structural support for the capillary loops. The straight cylinder is a geometry example for both settings, not a depiction of a glomerular loop or an estimate of how much surface either cell contacts. The dimensions are invented for teaching, not measurements. The model does not describe blood flow, filtration, or the effects of cell contraction.";
"health_atlas_capillary_support_surface_title" = "Model capillary side area";
"health_atlas_ependymal_beat_phase_formula" = "Unwrapped phase difference (cycles) = toy frequency (cycles per interval) × timing offset (intervals). The wrapped value keeps only the fractional part, so every whole cycle returns to the same phase as zero offset.";
"health_atlas_ependymal_beat_phase_frequency" = "Toy beat frequency";
"health_atlas_ependymal_beat_phase_offset" = "Toy timing offset";
"health_atlas_ependymal_beat_phase_scope" = "Motile cilia on the ependymal lining of the brain ventricles can help move nearby cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). This model compares two repeating beats with the same invented frequency and a chosen timing offset. The intervals are fictional, not seconds. The model does not predict real beat rates or CSF flow and does not guide interventions.";
"health_atlas_ependymal_beat_phase_title" = "Ependymal beat phase difference";
"health_atlas_ependymal_beat_phase_wrapped" = "Wrapped phase difference: %1$@ cycles";
"health_atlas_gamete_path_distance_companion" = "Starting separation: %1$@ PU";
"health_atlas_gamete_path_distance_formula" = "Remaining distance (PU) = starting separation − sperm icon shift − oocyte icon shift.";
"health_atlas_gamete_path_distance_oocyte_shift" = "Oocyte icon shift to the left";
"health_atlas_gamete_path_distance_scope" = "Sperm can propel themselves with a flagellum. The oocyte does not propel itself: cilia-driven fluid movement and smooth muscle contractions help carry it through the uterine tube. On one imagined line, a sperm icon starts at 0 and shifts right; an oocyte icon starts at 16 and shifts left. Their shifts are chosen independently in invented position units (PU). The icons stay apart, and the model does not represent real paths or rates or predict fertilization or fertility.";
"health_atlas_gamete_path_distance_sperm_shift" = "Sperm icon shift to the right";
"health_atlas_gamete_path_distance_title" = "Distance between gamete icons";
"health_atlas_marrow_renewal_added" = "Icons added by renewal";
"health_atlas_marrow_renewal_companion" = "Ending icon count: %1$@";
"health_atlas_marrow_renewal_formula" = "Net icon change = icons added by renewal minus icons that leave. Ending count = starting count + net change. With the number leaving fixed, each extra renewal icon raises the net change by one. The chart rounds to whole icons.";
"health_atlas_marrow_renewal_left" = "Starting icons that leave";
"health_atlas_marrow_renewal_scope" = "Blood stem cells (hematopoietic stem cells) are found in bone marrow. They can self-renew and give rise to different blood-cell lineages. For this exercise, a fictitious compartment starts with 12 icons. Over one example interval, renewal adds icons and some of the starting icons can leave. These invented counts are for arithmetic only and do not represent actual cells, biological rates, or movement out of marrow.";
"health_atlas_marrow_renewal_title" = "Net change in marrow icons";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_caption" = "Relative sliding across the film";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_diagram" = "The chest-wall side moves relative to the lung side. The film is magnified for illustration. Model shear rate: %1$@ per second.";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_formula" = "Shear rate = relative speed ÷ film thickness. Multiplying that rate by an example viscosity of 0.01 Pa·s gives the model shear stress.";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_lung" = "Lung side";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_pause" = "Pause pleural sliding";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_play" = "Play pleural sliding";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_scope" = "Pleural fluid helps the two pleural surfaces slide during breathing. This example uses ideal parallel surfaces and a fictional fluid viscosity; the lower surface is the model reference. It does not measure pleural fluid, pressure, or breathing performance.";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_speed" = "Model sliding speed";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_stress" = "Model shear stress: %1$@ Pa";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_thickness" = "Model film thickness";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_title" = "Pleural sliding: model shear rate";
"health_atlas_pleural_slide_wall" = "Chest-wall side";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_coefficient" = "Model absorption coefficient";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_companion" = "Absorbed light: %1$@ a.u.";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_diagram" = "The colored layer represents the chosen path length and attenuation; the fading outgoing rays represent the transmitted light.";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_formula" = "The beam starts with 100 model light units. For a path length d and an absorption coefficient k, the transmitted amount is 100 × exp(−k × d). At a fixed coefficient, each equal stretch of the path absorbs the same fraction of the light entering that stretch. With no reflection or scattering in this model, the absorbed amount is 100 minus the transmitted amount.";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_incident" = "Incident light";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_layer" = "Example absorbing layer";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_path_length" = "Path length in the model layer";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_scope" = "Cone photoreceptors convert light into changes in their electrical signals, a process called phototransduction. Melanin in cells of the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) absorbs stray light and reduces internal reflections. These are distinct roles. The plot shows only absorption of a fictional beam by an invented, uniform layer, with all values in arbitrary model units. It does not model either cell type, retinal sensitivity, or macular pigment optical density.";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_title" = "Transmitted light from a fictional beam";
"health_atlas_retinal_light_attenuation_transmitted" = "Transmitted light";
"health_atlas_testis_tubule_section_companion" = "Lumen area: %1$@ u²";
"health_atlas_testis_tubule_section_formula" = "With outer radius r and wall thickness t, the inner radius is r − t. Inner area = π × (r − t)². Wall share = 100 × [1 − ((r − t) / r)²], expressed as a percentage of the whole section.";
"health_atlas_testis_tubule_section_radius" = "Outer radius";
"health_atlas_testis_tubule_section_scope" = "In the testis, spermatogonia lie along the basement membrane of seminiferous tubules. Spermatocytes are a later germ-cell stage and are found farther inward as they develop, still within the epithelium. Sertoli cells extend from the basement membrane to the lumen and support developing germ cells. Leydig cells lie outside the tubule in the interstitial tissue between tubules, so they are not included in either area. The model simplifies one tubule to a circular section with a uniform wall. At a fixed outer radius, a thicker wall takes up more of the section and leaves less inner area. The dimensions are invented and use arbitrary units (u). The model does not measure real cell size or count, assess fertility, or predict assay results.";
"health_atlas_testis_tubule_section_thickness" = "Wall thickness";
"health_atlas_testis_tubule_section_title" = "Wall share of a seminiferous tubule cross-section";
"health_atlas_uterine_lining_area_companion" = "Model inner circumference: %1$@ LU";
"health_atlas_uterine_lining_area_formula" = "Model side area (LU²) = 2π × inner radius (LU) × length (LU). At a fixed length, a larger radius gives more area. At a fixed radius, a longer cylinder gives more area.";
"health_atlas_uterine_lining_area_length" = "Model cylinder length";
"health_atlas_uterine_lining_area_radius" = "Model inner radius";
"health_atlas_uterine_lining_area_scope" = "Endometrial epithelial cells cover the inner surface of the uterine body as part of the endometrium. The cervical canal has its own mucus-secreting columnar lining, not endometrium. Both have an inner surface, like a lining inside an open tube. This model counts only the curved inner wall of a smooth cylinder, not either end. Its dimensions use invented length units (LU) and do not represent the shape or size of either anatomical space.";
"health_atlas_uterine_lining_area_title" = "Uterine lining area: a cylinder analogy";
"health_atlas_vulvar_fold_overlap_angle" = "Model outer-flap angle";
"health_atlas_vulvar_fold_overlap_companion" = "Outer flap projected reach: %1$@%% of the reference span";
"health_atlas_vulvar_fold_overlap_formula" = "On a reference line from 0 to 1, the fixed inner flap spans 0.25 to 0.75. The outer flap starts at 0. Its projected reach is P = L * cos(angle), where L is its length relative to the reference span and the angle is measured from the line. The plot shows the percentage of the inner flap covered in projection: 100 * (P - 0.25) / 0.50, limited to the range 0 to 100.";
"health_atlas_vulvar_fold_overlap_length" = "Outer flap length / reference span";
"health_atlas_vulvar_fold_overlap_scope" = "The labia majora provide outer covering. The labia minora are distinct inner folds that border and help protect the vestibule. The two flaps are hypothetical: projected overlap does not represent tissue motion, contact, or degree of protection. The settings and results do not describe actual dimensions, pressure, sensitivity, or individual appearance, and cannot support clinical inference.";
"health_atlas_vulvar_fold_overlap_title" = "Vulvar fold overlap (model)";
